> So the only way to identify a zi'hevla is by stress? e.g. `cpiku ku` vs.
> `cpikuku`

You tell that a word is in greater brivla space (a term from my program=20
valfendi) because it ends in a vowel and its second consonant is either nex=
t=20
to another consonant or separated by "y" from a consonant. If you have stre=
ss=20
but not word divisions (i.e. you're hearing speech), the word division of a=
=20
brivla is such that the next-to-last syllable (not counting syllables whose=
=20
nucleus is "y" or a consonant) is stressed and the last syllable ends in a=
=20
vowel. You tell a zi'evla from a gismu because it is not a gismu or sequenc=
e=20
of more than one rafsi. (A single rafsi either doesn't have two consonants =
or=20
doesn't have two vowels.)

"cp=C3=ADkuku" is a gismu and a cmavo: "cpiku ku". "cpik=C3=BAku" is a zi'e=
vla.
"dadysabodre" is in greater brivla space but is not a word. Since it has "y=
"=20
in it, it's a lujvo if it's a brivla; but it cannot be broken into rafsi.

